h becomes mediated by capital through desiring-production and its connection to capitalism. They also reference Marx’s analysis of how labor, money-capital, and production circuits interact, and how differentiation and critique of capitalist social arrangements arise from these dynamics. Additionally, the material alludes to the apparatus of capture and the triadic relation among land, labor, and money as central components of Marx’s framework. A provocative note appears in one description, suggesting that suffering can be a form of self-enjoyment within desiring-production, reflecting a critical interpretation of the interplay between desire, value, and capitalist production. Overall, the summaries position Karl Marx as a foundational figure whose theories illuminate the transformation of labor into capital, the capitalist mode of production, and the complexeconomy of property relations, while connecting these ideas to broader discussions of social theory and critique.", "BERNARD SCHMITT:Bernard Schmitt proposed a model of the apparatus of capture that emphasizes operations of comparison and appropriation, and connects money to capitalist economics within the framework described in the text", "MARX:MARX refers to Karl Marx, a 19th-century German philosopher and economist, and a central figure in Marxist theory. The descriptions identify him as the author of Capital and as the origin of foundational ideas about capitalism, money, and labor. MARX is cited as the observer of the fundamental opposition between capital and labor and as describing capital’s role in extracting surplus labor. His analysis of political economy is noted, including the abstract subjective essence of wealth produced through labor, production, and desire, all discussed within the broader themes of capitalism’s deterritorialization and reterritorialization. The texts also emphasize MARX’s relevance to money, credit, and capitalist accumulation, as well as his critique of how monetary relations underpin capitalistic expansion. Additionally, MARX is linked to discussions of state violence within capitalist formations, implying how capital relations influence coercive or violent state mechanisms. A separate point references MARX’s formulation that humanity “only sets itself problems that it is capable of solving,” used to contrast with Bergson’s ideas on practice and problem-setting. Taken together, these descriptions present MARX as a foundational philosopher-economist whose work on capital, labor, money, and the dynamics of capitalism—including its spatial transformations and the state’s role—provides a coherent framework for understanding political economy and social power relations.", "LEWIS MUMFORD:Renowned American historian, sociologist, and critic who wrote on urban theory, technology, and culture; cited in this text as supporting the designation that archaic empires are megamachines.", "REULEAUX:Franz Reuleaux, 19th-century German engineer and founder of the science of mechanism; referenced in the text for the classic definition of a machine.", "JOHN KENNETH GALBRAITH:John Kenneth Galbraith is an American economist and public intellectual; in this text he is cited alongside Francois Chatelet as a co-formulator of the concept of constant and decisive errors, referenced within discussions on capitalism, state power, and axiomatics", "FRANCOIS CHATELET:Francois Chatelet is a French philosopher and political theorist; in this text he is cited with John Kenneth Galbraith as a co-formulator of the concept of constant and decisive errors, contributing to analysis of capitalism, the state, and axiomatics", "BRAZIL:A country in South America; used in the text as an example illustrating the mobility of axioms and the tension between totalitarianism and social democracy within the capitalist framework.", "PIERRE BOULEZ:Pierre Boulez is a French composer and conductor who originated key theoretical concepts in musical space organization, notably the distinction between smooth and striated space, the notion of module distribution of breaks and intervals, and the relations between rhythm, pitch, and perception; he created and described these concepts in the field of music to account for abstract distinctions alongside concrete musical practices.)<|COMPLETE|>", "BERGSON:Henri Bergson, a French philosopher (1859–1941) and the central figure in Bergsonism, is celebrated for developing a dynamic, non-mechanistic account of time, memory, and life. His core idea is durée, the inner, qualitative flow of time that cannot be reduced to spatial measurement or numerical clock-time. Duration is a multi-velocity multiplicity in which moments differ in kind and degree, and it provides the living basis for perception, memory, and consciousness. Bergson argues that memory is not a static repository but a living, active principle—memory is duration, and consciousness is coextensive with life. Perception and recollection intertwine through active and passive syntheses, with recollection moving through planes of consciousness and transforming into recollection-images via processes such as translation, contraction (detendus), and expansion. The past thus persists within the present and continually informs how experience unfolds.\n\nA key aspect of Bergson’s thought is the elan vital, a creative, differentiating force that drives life beyond mere mechanism. This creative vitalism posits that life actualizes virtual totalities through novelty, opening up new planes of nature and instruments of freedom. Time, for Bergson, resists strict spatialization; matter and mind are interwoven in a continuum, and explanations based on rigid dualisms or finalistic schemes are insufficient. He promotes intuition as the methodological key to knowing and solving problems—an approach that grasps the immediacy of experience rather than reducing it to abstract analysis. Problems are best understood by intrinsic formulation and by following divergent lines of fact toward a virtual convergence point, which yields a sufficient reason for departure points.\n\nBergson also develops important technical distinctions, such as qualitative versus numerical multiplicities, and the distinction between the smooth and the striated, which he uses to critique purely mechanistic conceptions of time and space. His mature philosophy emphasizes that the Whole is virtual and that duration, memory, and consciousness unfold in complexity without being captured by simple formulas. In his major works—Matter and Memory, Creative Evolution, and Time and Free Will—he explores how memory, sensation, and intention interrelate; he challenges the notion that the real must resemble the possible and argues that genuine life creates novelty through creative evolution.\n\nThus, Henri Bergson’s doctrine—Bergsonism—centers on durée, memory, intuition, and elan vital, offering a comprehensive framework that unites time, consciousness, and creative life in opposition to deterministic or purely material explanations.", "EINSTEIN:Albert Einstein is the central figure described, referenced as the namesake of the Einstein abstract machine in the text. He is portrayed as a renowned 20th-century theoretical physicist who developed the theory of relativity (both special and general) and made pivotal contributions to quantum theory, including the famous equation E=mc^2, and he was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921. The descriptions also note that Einstein is referenced within the Physics section of the text. In discussions of time, Einstein’s concept of simultaneity and the relativity of time are presented and critiqued in contrast to Bergson’s views. Additionally, Einstein is depicted as having discussions or confrontations with Bergson on the topic of Relativity, with the dialogue positioned within the context of Riemannian multiplicities, signaling a mathematical frame for evaluating his theories. Overall, the entries converge on Einstein as the principal figure associated with relativity and quantum contributions, while also situating him in philosophical and mathematical discourse with Bergson.", "DEMOCRITUS:Democritus, an Ancient Greek philosopher, is credited as the first to articulate atomism; his ideas about indivisible atoms form the basis of the atomistic framework referenced in the passage, and he is identified as the starting point in the ancient lineage that culminates with Lucretius. The text also invokes his atomistic theory, noting that he fragmented Parmenides’ One-Being and multiplied it into atoms."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_5256bd08119d4af56989874369dfd2ee99c28e2cff4c0848b30218246bc266c5_v2"}